===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|colloquial}}

# {{lb|en|British}} To [[wander]] around [[aimlessly]], often causing irritation to others.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J. S. Fletcher|authorlink=J. S. Fletcher|title=The Middle of Things|year=1922|publisher=A. Knopf|location=New York|page=161|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/middleofthings00fletuoft/page/161/mode/1up|passage=These chaps that '''mooch''' about, as Hyde was doing, pick up all sorts of odds and ends. He may have pinched them from a chemist’s shop.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=8 May 2019|author=Barney Ronay|title=Liverpool’s waves of red fury and recklessness end in joyous bedlam|work=The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/07/liverpool-fury-recklessness-bedlam-champions-league-barcelona|passage=With 79 minutes gone, the most celebrated team of the modern age had been reduced to bunch of '''mooching''', stumbling yellow-shirted spectators.}}
# To [[beg]], [[cadge]], or [[sponge]]; to [[exploit]] or [[take advantage]] of others for personal gain.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:scrounge}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|page=26|author=Michael L. Frankel & friends|title=Gently with the Tides|publisher=Center for Marine Conservation|location=Washington (DC)|ISBN=1879269-007|passage=I managed to '''mooch''' my way up the journalistic ladder to the next, more impressive level of “Interviewer”.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Andrew R. Chow|quotee={{w|Richard Prince}}|title=Copyright Case Over Richard Prince Instagram Show to Go Forward|work=The New York Times|date=2017-07-20|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/07/20/arts/design/richard-prince-instagram-copyright-lawsuit.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Mr. Prince responded on Twitter: “Phony fraud photographers keep '''mooching''' me. Why? I changed the game,” he wrote on Wednesday. His Instagram account, which previously had over 70,000 followers, is currently disabled.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|mostly|British}} To [[steal]] or [[filch]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:steal}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Susan Alice Bickford|title=Dread of Winter|passage=I'm tired of driving you all over and sick of you living in my house, '''mooching''' my food.}}

